---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, GITHUB_URL, termphinUrl } from '../data/site';

const path = '/about';
const title = 'About sshkeygen.dev - Open-Source SSH Key Generator';
const description =
  'sshkeygen.dev is a free, MIT-licensed SSH key generator that runs entirely in your browser. Who makes it, how it works, and how to verify and self-host it.';
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  crumb="About"
  heading="About sshkeygen.dev"
  lead="An SSH key generator that runs entirely in your browser, plus the guides to put the key to use."
  articleType="AboutPage"
  cta={false}
>
  <p>
    Most people should create keys with <a href="/ssh-keygen">ssh-keygen</a>. This site is for when there is no
    terminal at hand: a locked-down laptop, a tablet, a colleague who has never opened a shell. Many web generators
    create keys on a server or ship trackers. This one writes the same OpenSSH files as ssh-keygen and never sends
    them anywhere.
  </p>

  <h2 id="how">How it is built</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A static site with no server-side code.</li>
    <li>
      Randomness from the Web Crypto API. RSA and ECDSA use the browser's native implementation; Ed25519 uses
      <code>@noble/curves</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The OpenSSH key format, aes256-ctr with bcrypt_pbkdf, fingerprints and randomart are written in TypeScript and
      tested against the real ssh-keygen.
    </li>
    <li>A Content Security Policy that allows no network connections and no third-party resources.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="open-source">Open source</h2>
  <p>
    The source is on <a href={GITHUB_URL} rel="noopener">GitHub</a> under the MIT license. Build it yourself, host it
    on your own domain or run it offline. Report security problems privately to
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who">Who makes it</h2>
  <p>
    The team behind <a href={termphinUrl('about')} rel="noopener">Termphin</a>, an SSH client whose sessions are never
    killed. When you lock your phone, switch from Wi-Fi to mobile data or lose the connection, a small helper on the
    server keeps the shell running and Termphin puts you back where you left off, screen included, without a tmux
    setup. It also has an SFTP file browser, snippets, port forwarding and jump hosts, keeps keys in an encrypted vault,
    and is completely free. Unlike this site, the Termphin app is not open source; the helper it uploads to your server
    and its terminal engine are.
  </p>
  <p>
    sshkeygen.dev stays free and works without Termphin. Questions and corrections:
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> or a <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}/issues`} rel="noopener">GitHub
    issue</a>.
  </p>
</Article>
